Hardware and Desktop Support
Overview

During my IT support experience at The Documented Warehouse, I provided hands-on hardware and desktop support for laptops, desktops,
thin clients, and ASUS NUC devices.
Support included troubleshooting hardware problems, replacing components, upgrading SSDs and RAM, installing Windows and drivers, 
and preparing machines for deployment.

Hardware Troubleshooting

When a user reported that a machine was not working, I first gathered information about the reported problem and physically inspected the 
device when it was available at my location.
For branch users, I gathered as much information as possible before deciding whether the device or a specific component needed to be sent to
our location for further investigation.

1.Battery Troubleshooting-Test with a different charger to help isolate the problem.
                         -Check whether the device receives power.
                         -Remove the battery where possible and test the device on the charger.
                         -Test and charge the battery where applicable.
                         -Determine whether the issue is related to the battery, charger, or another power-related problem.
                         -Where a faulty battery could be confirmed, a replacement could sometimes be ordered and sent directly to the branch.

 2.RAM Troubleshooting-Inspect the RAM and RAM slots when investigating a machine that is not working.
                      -Clean dust from RAM slots where required.
                      -Test the existing RAM.
                      -Test with another RAM module where available.
                      -Replace the RAM when a faulty module is identified and a replacement is available.
                      -Order the required RAM when it is not available internally.

3.Keyboard Support-Replace keyboards on desktops and thin clients when required.
                  -For laptops, identify the laptop model and assess how the keyboard is installed.
                  -Determine how much of the laptop needs to be opened to access the keyboard.
                  -Replace laptop keyboards internally when the repair is suitable to perform.
                  -Send more complicated laptop keyboard repairs to TechExpress.

4.SSD Upgrades and Data Migration

SSD upgrades were performed internally on the machines I supported.

Depending on the machine and user setup, different methods were used to preserve the existing environment:

SSD Cloning-Install the old and new SSDs into the cloning machine.
           -Select the cloning option and start the cloning process.
           -Monitor the cloning progress until completion.
           -Install the new SSD into the computer.
           -Power on the machine and confirm that it boots.
           -Perform a full functional check to confirm that the required environment is working.

5.Veeam Backup and Restore-Create a backup using Veeam.
                          -Restore the required environment from the Veeam backup.
                          -Boot the machine and perform functional checks.
                          -Confirm that the user's required data, applications, connectivity, and company software are working.

The approach depended on the type of machine and user environment. For RDWeb users, the local SSD migration process was different 
because of the way their environment was used.

6.ASUS NUC Assembly

I assembled ASUS NUC 15CRK devices that were supplied with components such as RAM and SSDs not yet installed.

The process included -Opening the NUC.
                     -Installing the RAM.
                     -Installing the SSD.
                     -Assembling the device.
                     -Installing Windows.
                     -Setting up Windows offline.
                     -Installing required device drivers, including Wi-Fi drivers.
                     -Installing required software according to the company SOP.

7.Windows Installation and Desktop Preparation-Created bootable Windows USB drives using Rufus.
                                              -Installed Windows on supported machines.
                                              -Checked the Windows edition before commissioning the machine.
                                              -Confirmed that machines had Windows Pro as required.
                                              -Installed required drivers.
                                              -Installed required company software according to the SOP.
                                              -Installed and configured required tools such as ESET, Mimecast, AnyDesk, Microsoft 365/Office, and BitLocker

8.Machine Commissioning

Before a machine could be tagged and commissioned, I checked that the Windows installation met the required specification.

I then followed the company SOP to prepare the machine for deployment and completed the required commissioning and asset tagging process.

External Repairs and Escalation

When a hardware repair was not suitable to perform internally, the device was sent to TechExpress for further repair.

This included assessing whether a repair could safely and practically be completed internally before arranging an external repair.


